# Canonical sizing + per-session budgets for phased-execution.
# SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH (F5): scripts/phase-graph.sh sources this file, and
# references/sizing.md documents these exact numbers. Change them here only.
#
# Plain KEY=VALUE (sourced by bash 3.2) — no spaces around '=', integers in tokens.

# Working-set weight of a phase by Size tag (tokens).
SIZE_S=15000
SIZE_M=40000
SIZE_L=90000

# Per-session working-set budget by model class (tokens of summed phase WEIGHT,
# not raw context). Budget ~= 0.2 x effective window, so a session's context
# TARGET — 60% of the window, SESSION_TARGET_PCT below — is 3x its budget: clear
# of auto-compaction (~83%) and of late-window quality rot. What a session's
# context actually is comes from the session model below, never from a multiple
# of its weight (control-tower phase 59, #83).
BUDGET_HAIKU=40000      # 200K-window cheap/fast model → 0.2 × 200K
BUDGET_BIG=200000       # opus / sonnet / fable / mythos → 0.2 × 1M of WEIGHT; its context is the session model below
BUDGET_DEFAULT=40000    # unknown / unspecified model → assume a 200K effective window
BUDGET_1M=200000        # any model carrying the [1m] window suffix (scripts/models.env)

# A note on BUDGET_BIG, kept deliberately rather than silently corrected.
#
# Its old comment asserted "(1M window)" for the bare aliases. That is an
# assumption, not a measurement: the CLI takes a [1m] suffix precisely because
# a bare alias may resolve to a smaller window, and it says so itself when it
# meets a model it does not recognise ("append [1m] to the model name for 1M").
# BUDGET_1M is therefore the only rating in this file the console can actually
# defend, and it is separated out so the two can move independently.
#
# Re-rating the bare aliases down to a 200K window would be more honest and
# would change how EVERY existing plan batches, so it is an operator decision
# and not a refactor. The single edit is BUDGET_BIG above; nothing else in the
# tree needs to change, because models.env now says which alias is in which
# class and phase-graph.sh and analysis/graph.ts both read it from there.

# ---- The session model (control-tower phase 59, #83 / AUD-25) --------------
#
# A fresh phase session's PEAK context, measured rather than assumed:
#
#     peak ≈ SESSION_BOOT_FLOOR + SESSION_WORK_FLOOR + SESSION_SLOPE_PCT/100 × weight
#
# The old model said "real ≈ 3 × weight", which is a session that costs nothing
# before its work begins. Measured, the FIRST call alone reads the boot prefix
# (system prompt, tools, CLAUDE.md, rules, memory, boot prompt) and the tags
# barely separate: 107 of 108 audited phases exceeded the 3 × line.
#
#   SESSION_BOOT_FLOOR  the first call's context — per REPOSITORY, measured by
#                       the console from `phase.tokens` firstContext; this is the
#                       shipped value, the median of 205 fresh phase sessions on
#                       two consoles (121K; the smaller repository booted at 89K).
#   SESSION_WORK_FLOOR  what every phase session adds above its boot, whatever its
#                       size — the fitted floor (319,243) less the boot.
#   SESSION_SLOPE_PCT   context per token of weight, in hundredths (2.17).
#
# Fitted by viewer/server/analysis/sizing-model.ts `fitContextModel` — per-size
# median peaks, every tag weighing once — over the 264 fresh phase sessions of
# at least 20 calls in viewer/test/fixtures/sizing/sessions-corpus.json
# (2026-09-16 → 2026-09-25): median S 325K, M 447K, L 501K, each within 10% of
# the line. test/sizing-model.test.ts re-fits the corpus and holds these numbers
# to it. `boot + work` is paid ONCE per session, which is what a batch check adds.
SESSION_BOOT_FLOOR=121000
SESSION_WORK_FLOOR=198000
SESSION_SLOPE_PCT=217
# The share of the window a session is sized to stay under: the console's own
# wrap-up line (viewer/server/runner/usage.ts CONTEXT_WRAPUP_FRACTION). A model's
# window is 5 × its budget above, so the target is 3 × the budget.
SESSION_TARGET_PCT=60

# ---- Sessions per phase under the console: 1 phase ≥ 1 session --------------
#
# The console's autopilot never batches (#83 SIZ-3): every phase gets at least a
# session of its own, and a phase that wraps its context (the 60% steer, the 80%
# checkpoint) takes more. So a plan's forecast in sessions is the sum, over the
# phases left, of what phases of each size measurably took — never its weight
# over a budget, which forecast 0.35–0.67 sessions a phase against a measured
# 1.1–5.0.
#
#   SESSIONS_<S|M|L>_X100       mean sessions of a phase that did NOT wrap, × 100
#   SESSIONS_<S|M|L>_WRAP_X100  mean sessions of a phase that DID wrap, × 100
#   WRAP_<S|M|L>_PCT            the share of phases of that size that wrapped
#
# Measured by sizing-model.ts `sessionsPerPhaseOf` over the 156 finished phases of
# the same corpus, each count winsorised at 5 (one ten-session S doubled the S
# mean on its own). A phase is expected to take
# ((100 − WRAP) × SESSIONS + WRAP × SESSIONS_WRAP) / 10000 sessions: S 1.71,
# M 1.66, L 1.93.
SESSIONS_S_X100=169
SESSIONS_S_WRAP_X100=200
WRAP_S_PCT=7
SESSIONS_M_X100=146
SESSIONS_M_WRAP_X100=278
WRAP_M_PCT=15
SESSIONS_L_X100=148
SESSIONS_L_WRAP_X100=254
WRAP_L_PCT=42
